+# `targets:` of every vendor in platform/vendors/consoles.yaml whose store_default is human-vault.
+# Edit the registry, never this file; bin/idp-ci fails when this file and a fresh render differ.
+#
+# The human door, wired (decision 0017). A key the founder drops into Bitwarden Secrets Manager
+# under the name in a target's `bw:` field lands in the cluster as a Secret named human-<vendor>,
+# in the namespace that consumes it, within the refresh interval. Nothing is pasted into a portal
+# and no value is ever typed into this repository.
+#
+# Why one ExternalSecret per vendor rather than one per namespace holding every key: External
+# Secrets fails an ExternalSecret whole when any one of its `data` entries names a secret the store
+# does not hold. Split this way, a vendor whose key has not been dropped in yet fails only its own
+# Secret, and its status line names exactly which key is missing -- so the set of failing bridge
+# ExternalSecrets IS the list of keys still to add, with no separate inventory to keep true.
+#
+# Addressing is by name, not by Bitwarden id, and that is load-bearing. `dataFrom.find` was
+# measured on 2026-09-07 against bitwarden-sdk-server v0.6.0 and it IGNORES `name.regexp`: a find
+# for `^NO_SUCH_KEY_AT_ALL$` returned the project's unrelated secret and `rewrite` then renamed it
+# onto the field. A bridge built on find would quietly serve one vendor's value under every other
+# vendor's key. `data[].remoteRef.key: <name>` resolves exactly, and refuses when the name is
+# absent, which is the behaviour a credential path must have.
